it's great to be joined today once again  by economist richard wolf who is  professor emeritus of economics at my  alma mater the University of  Massachusetts in Amherst currently a  visiting professor at the new school  university in new york city so professor  what we've been starting to dig in to  Donald Trump's hundred day plan and  really specifically looking at the  pieces of that plan that relate to the  economy and sort of comparing and  contrasting whether those policies if  made effective would make America great  again as the phrase goes particularly  for the middle class which is a group  that Donald Trump really tried to appeal  to so there's a number of areas we could  look at on this his claims about  withdrawing from NAFTA and starting  trade wars tax policy we've already seen  that his tax cuts are not going to be  nearly as big as he initially said and  actually the lowest tax bracket is going  to go from ten to twelve percent  whatever angle you think is the most  relevant and analysis for the middle  class I'm glad to start there well let  me give you what may not be the welcome  news it is that an honest appraisal of  what has gone on so far is such a bundle  of contradictions that I would be beware  of anyone who claims to say it will  unbalance the positive or negative for  this group or that group contradictions  are so enormous that even if you read  closely some of the pieces that are  headlined as though they know which way  it's going when you read down into the  body of the article if it's a decent  reporter or commentator they will say  but you know it could be and then they  basically tell you it could be the  opposite of what the first part of the  article assertive and I think that's a  flaw in the reporter I think that's  because mr. Trump either doesn't know  how to formulate a governmental program  or doesn't want to reveal what it is  he's in fact going to do or doesn't much  care one of those because  it doesn't really compute let me give  you some examples yeah he names a fellow  from the South priced to be his health  secretary a man who's committed  apparently above all else in life to  undo the Affordable Care Act often known  as Obamacare and when he summarizes what  he's going to do he's going to quote  unquote let the market decide in other  words not to cover people as a right as  a feature of citizenship in the United  States the way most other industrial  countries do but rather to let supply  and demand and profit-making companies  produce whatever outcome they tend to  produce it's the market idea which has  usually been a venir for self-serving of  the business community anyway but here  the remarkable thing is the same day  that he announces mr. prices selection  he also announces with great excitement  that he and vice president-elect pence  are heading off to Indiana having  muscled he claims the carrier  corporation into not making the market  dictated decision of moving about a  thousand jobs to Mexico because what the  market dictates to that company is  precisely what mr. Trump is there to  prevent happening well on the one hand  we're gonna let the market decide on the  other hand we're not officially but  let's pause there for a second cuz this  gets into something that's so  interesting it and yes you know we're  gonna get deep delve really deeply into  the entire carrier thing uh tomorrow so  I'm seeing in the proposals a sort of  unusual mix of carrot and stick policies  so a carrot policy would be for example  reducing the corporate tax rate and  taxes on the rich to try to sort of lure  them with the carrot as the phrase might  be uh to make investments in new  businesses or the it's basically  supplies  I trickle-down economics and at the same  time you have stick ideas that this idea  that Trump floated which I guess now  he's going to back off of of a very  harsh penalty for companies that move  jobs out of the u.s. to other countries  is this relatively unusual this  combination of carrot and stick policies  to be floated or is there something  interesting to explore there that maybe  isn't often looked at well again it's  possible that mr. Trump and his advisors  really are prepared to muscle and  threaten our companies that do not make  market based decisions of the sort they  think will make them look good  politically it's possible it has not  been the behavior of most governments in  the United States over the last  half-century neither Republican nor  democrat uh they sometimes talk a little  bit about doing all of these things but  the reality time and again has been to  basically not offend corporate America  for fear of the political consequences  of doing it there is a toleration among  the corporations for the language they  know that campaigns have rhetoric that  goes over the top they expect it they're  not badly thrown by it they assume it's  mostly a froth that will subside once  the election is over and i think mr.  Trump having narrowly to say the least  won this race would not be in a strong  position to do something that might  really unite corporate America against  what he is and what he represents would  be a strange thing for a man in his  position to do given our history and  given his vulnerability let's talk a  little bit about trade deals many times  trade deals are not really trade deals  they're multinational corporate power  and ability to skirt national law  deals but to the extent that trade deals  are trade deals we are now starting to  see more and more pieces be published by  economists and journalists and  commentators saying you know up the  actual trade deals that are currently in  place do keep down the costs of many  products for Americans and what Donald  Trump is sort of ignoring is that  although he might uh make himself feel  good for keeping a campaign promise he  may or may not have understood when he  made it by starting to get out of trade  deals he sees as unfavorable for  Americans this is going to have there's  another side of the coin to these trade  deals isn't there so can you talk a  little bit about that I mean are there  if there is a trade war first of all  does anybody win and number two are  there ways in which these trade deals  are good for Americans that is not  really being discussed absolutely the  answer to both of them is that there are  trade deals and then again there are  trade deals nothing is easier than to  look at a deal that often takes fifty  hundred two hundred pages to lay out the  specifics of and find something you  don't like you think we could have done  better you will do better this is  theater this is not serious work a trade  deal usually involves many partners and  even if it's only two partners bilateral  trade deals it's a matter of give and  take like most deals you get this  advantage but you give up that advantage  otherwise the partner doesn't make the  deal with you and and that's the normal  situation has the United States  benefited from trade deals the answer is  yes and no some industries have done  real well others haven't the industries  that suffer when we open our borders the  production abroad have disappeared in  the United States we have a much much  smaller automobile industry in America  than we used to because we import cars  or we let foreign companies set up  inside the United States the same is  true with computers the same is true  with cameras and a whole host of other  products those industries have either  disappeared in the United States or in  order to survive they moved production  abroad which is indeed what the carrier  corporation is facing as its problems it  can't compete unless it has lower wages  than it pays in the United States  confronting the United States with a  conundrum if you want to keep the  products cheap you have to lose the job  if you keep the jobs you have to be  prepared to pay very high prices and  basically all the wheeling and dealing  is about that struggle in terms of which  side will prevail because it has more  power one thing to add if mr. Trump had  the slightest intention to rhina go we  negotiate trade deals in such a way that  the American middle class is the  beneficiary then he would have to make  use of the apparatus of the trade union  movement in this country of academics in  this country who have been working and  advocating for such a solution for 50  years he never speaks to them he never  invites them he does not include them in  any of the negotiations or plans never  has makes no statement that he ever will  which means that to imagine that when  all the dust clears a billionaire  president weeb ago she ain't trade deals  will make sure because he wants us to  trust him that he will take care of  everybody without including any of the  putative beneficiaries in the process  last thing i want to touch on in the  limited time we have the one of these  hundred day plan items is to label china  a currency manipulator aside from  whether that's an accurate allegation  does that labeling actually have any  real-world impact in and of itself it  could because it could be the prelude to  being tariffs upon them something else  that mr. Trump said during the campaign  variously thirty five percent or forty  five percent to do any of this is to  declare economic warfare on the People's  Republic of China and to do that is to  take a very large step that in history  has often ended up in a transition from  economic warfare to the military kind  let's remind ourselves quickly half of  the goods that come into the United  States from China are produced by  subsidiaries of American corporations  that have set up shop there to cut them  out of the American market with a tariff  is a direct assault by Trump on the  biggest multinational corporations in  the United States who've invested at  least three hundred billion dollars to  produce in China for export to the  United States it's not really going to  happen do you think that's likely second  example the Chinese are the major  creditor of the United States they own  the People's Republic of China more US  Treasury bills obligations of the United  States than any other country on earth  the chaos that would erupt if they  stopped buying in effect lending to the  United States government is hard to  imagine but the power of it is  unmistakable do you really want to  provoke that kind of confrontation and  thirdly China is the second most  important economy in the world they  build up their industries over last 30  years by export you close the world's  largest export market to China you are  plunging that country into extreme  measures and they are a very powerful  nuclear armed society one really has to  scratch one's head and wonder whether  mr. Trump is just spinning his wheels or  whether he's crazy if he's actually  going to move in that kind of direction  well we may find out sooner than later  we've been speaking with economist  richard wolf always great having you on  thank you David look forward to  next time

